            make_q("What is Metadata Filtering in Vector Databases?", ["Filtering vectors by scalar metadata attributes (e.g. `user_id == '123' AND date >= 2024`) before or during approximate nearest neighbor search", "Deleting corrupted vector files", "Filtering out stopwords from metadata", "Renaming database columns"], 0, "Metadata filtering restricts vector search to authorized or relevant subsets based on structured relational tags."),
            make_q("What is the difference between Pre-Filtering and Post-Filtering in vector databases?", ["Pre-filtering applies metadata constraints before vector similarity calculation; post-filtering searches the whole vector space first and discards matches that fail metadata rules", "Pre-filtering is only for text; post-filtering is for images", "Post-filtering runs on CPUs; pre-filtering on GPUs", "They are identical in performance"], 0, "Pre-filtering guarantees metadata criteria are satisfied and restricts the search scope; post-filtering can return zero results if top vector matches all fail metadata checks."),
            make_q("What is ColBERT (Khattab & Zaharia, 2020)?", ["A late-interaction retrieval model that computes token-level vector representations and computes maximum similarity scores via MaxSim: sum_q max_d (q . d)", "A model that translates between English and French", "A sparse retrieval model based on BM25", "A video retrieval architecture"], 0, "ColBERT preserves rich token-level contextual embeddings while retaining near-bi-encoder search speed via efficient MaxSim matrix operations."),
            make_q("What is SPLADE (Formal et al., 2021)?", ["A neural sparse retrieval model that uses a masked language model to expand queries and documents into rich, sparse lexical representations over vocabulary tokens", "A vector database written in Rust", "A chunking library for PDFs", "A method for splitting datasets"], 0, "SPLADE learns sparse lexical representations with automatic term expansion, outperforming BM25 while executing on traditional inverted index engines."),
            make_q("What is GraphRAG (Edge et al., Microsoft 2024)?", ["Extracting knowledge graphs (entities, relations, claims) from raw text with an LLM and using community detection summaries to answer global corpus-wide questions", "A RAG system for social networks only", "Plotting RAG metrics on a graph", "A database using graph neural networks for image search"], 0, "GraphRAG builds structured knowledge graphs from unstructured text, enabling holistic answers to broad questions like 'What are the main themes of this entire corpus?'"),
            make_q("What is the impact of embedding dimension on vector database memory?", ["A 1536-dimensional float32 vector requires 6,144 bytes per vector; 1 million vectors consume ~6 GB of RAM just for raw vector data", "Embedding dimension has zero impact on memory", "Higher dimension always uses less memory", "Vectors are stored as 1-bit integers"], 0, "Memory scales linearly with vector dimension: dim * 4 bytes per vector (e.g. 1536 * 4 = 6.144 KB per vector)."),
            make_q("What is Matryoshka Representation Learning (MRL, Kusupati et al., 2022)?", ["Training embedding models so that the first d dimensions (e.g. 256 of 1024) form valid, high-performing embeddings, allowing flexible dimension truncation to save 75% memory", "An embedding model developed in Russia", "A model with nested recursive layers", "A technique for stacking doll images"], 0, "MRL enforces information density into early dimensions, enabling practitioners to truncate vectors (e.g. 1536 -> 512) with negligible loss in retrieval recall."),
            make_q("What is Distance Metric selection in vector search (Euclidean L2 vs Cosine vs Inner Product)?", ["If vectors are L2-normalized, Cosine Similarity, Inner Product, and Euclidean distance produce identical ranking orders", "Inner Product only works on integers", "Cosine distance is non-linear", "Euclidean distance cannot run on GPUs"], 0, "For unit-normalized vectors: ||u - v||^2 = 2 - 2*(u . v). Thus, ranking by minimum Euclidean distance is mathematically identical to ranking by maximum dot product or cosine."),
